<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Teams' cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because most of its
    consumers are still in Base: DashboardService, AuditViewerService, ContactFieldService,
    the Shifts services, GoogleIntegration's TeamResourceService / GoogleAdminService /
    GoogleGroupSyncService / GoogleWorkspaceSyncService, AccountDeletionService and
    DuplicateAccountService all read through ITeamServiceRead or ITeamService, and
    HumansMetricsService, SystemTeamSyncJob, SuspendNonCompliantMembersJob and
    ProcessGoogleSyncOutboxJob do the same from Humans.Infrastructure. A Contracts/ *folder*
    inside Humans.Teams would therefore make Base reference a section and cycle
    (design §15.5b, memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    It holds exactly what lives outside the section: the read surface every other section
    injects, the ~20 members Base and four moved sections call on the full service, and the
    flat projections those signatures name. Everything else — the directory, detail, admin
    list, roster, join-request, role-management and early-entry DTOs — is internal.

    G5 lane 4b-2k brought this section's own vocabulary home from Humans.Domain:
    TeamMemberRole, RolePeriod, TeamJoinRequestStatus, SlotPriority, CallToActionStyle and
    the CallToAction value object all live here now, because the leaf's own signatures name
    them (Budget's rule). Measured, not assumed: CallToAction/CallToActionStyle/SlotPriority
    were filed under Camps and Shifts in the #866 appendix and have no consumer outside Teams.

    G5 lane 3a-1 added one non-Teams type on purpose: IActiveTeamsCacheInvalidator, still in
    namespace Humans.Application.Interfaces.Caching. Its six IMemoryCache-backed siblings went
    to Base, but this one's implementation injects ITeamService and had to land in the section,
    where HUM0034 makes it internal. Humans.Users injects the interface (AccountMergeService,
    NonCompliantMemberSuspension) and already referenced this leaf, so it costs zero csproj
    edits here or there; putting it on Humans.Teams would have forced a new Users → Teams edge.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService, SurfaceBudgetAttribute) and — new at
    G5 lane 3b (nobodies-collective/Humans#866) — Humans.Users.Contracts, for GoogleEmailStatus
    alone. That enum was the last thing keeping the old Humans.Domain edge alive here (lane
    3a-1 had already taken SystemTeamType and DomainConstants to Base); lane 3b emptied and
    deleted Humans.Domain and sent GoogleEmailStatus to its owner. Same single-enum dependency,
    repointed — not a new coupling to Users' service surface.

    Acyclic: Humans.Users.Contracts holds exactly one ProjectReference of its own
    (Humans.Onboarding.Contracts, which holds none), so the chain terminates in two hops. Note
    that Base references Humans.Users.Contracts, so nothing in that project may ever reference
    this one.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Auth.Contracts\Humans.Auth.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Users.Contracts\Humans.Users.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
